<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Black River (Asia)</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Da River, Sông Đà</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Black River, or Da River, rises in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and flows south-east through the mountains of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> to join the Red River. Its valley was home to Tai peoples organised in chiefly domains known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sip-song-chau-tai">Sip Song Chau Tai</a>, the twelve Tai lands, from which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> formed the Tai Federation in 1948.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/black-river-asia.jpg" alt="Black River (Asia)" width="320" height="195" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Laos 2003 CIA map.jpg: CIA derivative work: Bình Giang (talk) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hong_River.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
